## Write the rainfall data in increasing order
\text{Ordered} &: 47, 72, 84, 94, 94, 101, 133, 134, 149, 155
## Find the middle values
There are 10 values, so the median is the mean of the 5th and 6th.
\text{Median} &= \frac{94 + 101}{2}
&= \frac{195}{2}
&= 97.5 \text{ mm}
ANSWER: 97.5 \text{ mm}

## Add the sunshine hours
\text{Sum} &= 169+124+180+173+173+239+159+168+228+205
&= 1818
## Divide by the number of years
\text{Mean} &= \frac{1818}{10}
&= 181.8 \text{ hours}
ANSWER: 181.8 \text{ hours}

## Find the angle of the sector
$|\angle SQT| = 180^\circ - 149^\circ = 31^\circ$, and the radius is 8 m.
## Use the arc length formula
\text{Arc } TS &= 2\pi r \times \frac{\theta}{360}
&= 2\pi(8) \times \frac{31}{360}
&= \frac{62\pi}{45}
&= 4.3284
&\approx 4.3 \text{ m}
ANSWER: 4.3 \text{ m}

## Use the sector area formula
\text{Area} &= \pi r^2 \times \frac{\theta}{360}
&= \pi(8)^2 \times \frac{31}{360}
&= \frac{248\pi}{45}
&= 17.3136
&\approx 17.3 \text{ m}^2
ANSWER: 17.3 \text{ m}^2

## Multiply volume by 19.3 g per cm³
\text{Mass} &= 0.113 \times 19.3
&= 2.1809
&\approx 2.18 \text{ g}
ANSWER: 2.18 \text{ g}

## Atoms in 1 gram
197 \text{ g} &= 6.02 \times 10^{23} \text{ atoms}
1 \text{ g} &= \frac{6.02 \times 10^{23}}{197}
## Atoms in 2.18 grams
2.18 \text{ g} &= \frac{6.02 \times 10^{23}}{197} \times 2.18
&= 6.6617 \times 10^{21}
&\approx 6.7 \times 10^{21}
ANSWER: 6.7 \times 10^{21}

## Use the margin of error formula
\text{MOE} &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1560}}
&= 0.025318
&\approx 2.5\%
ANSWER: 2.5\%

## Differentiate $3x\ln x$ with the product rule
Product rule: $\frac{d}{dx}(uv) = u\,v' + v\,u'$ with $u=3x$, $v=\ln x$.
g'(x) &= 3x\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) + 3\ln x - 9 + 4\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)
## Simplify the derivative
&= 3 + 3\ln x - 9 + \frac{4}{x}
&= 3\ln x - 6 + \frac{4}{x}
## Substitute x = e
g'(e) &= 3\ln e - 6 + \frac{4}{e}
Since $\ln e = 1$:
&= 3 - 6 + \frac{4}{e}
&= -3 + \frac{4}{e}
## Evaluate to 2 decimal places
&= -1.53
ANSWER: g'(e) = -1.53

## Find the modulus r
r = |v| &= \sqrt{2^2 + (2\sqrt{3})^2}
&= \sqrt{4 + 12}
&= 4
## Find the argument
$v = 2 - 2\sqrt{3}\,i$ is in the fourth quadrant (real part positive, imaginary part negative).
\tan\alpha &= \frac{2\sqrt{3}}{2} = \sqrt{3}
\alpha &= \frac{\pi}{3}
\theta &= 2\pi - \frac{\pi}{3} = \frac{5\pi}{3}
## Write v in polar form
v &= 4\left(\cos\frac{5\pi}{3} + i\sin\frac{5\pi}{3}\right)
ANSWER: v = 4\left(\cos\frac{5\pi}{3} + i\sin\frac{5\pi}{3}\right)

## Collect the x terms on one side
x - \sqrt{32} &= \sqrt{128} - 5x
x + 5x &= \sqrt{128} + \sqrt{32}
## Write each surd in the form a root 2
\sqrt{128} &= \sqrt{64 \times 2} = 8\sqrt{2}
\sqrt{32} &= \sqrt{16 \times 2} = 4\sqrt{2}
## Substitute and solve
x + 5x &= 8\sqrt{2} + 4\sqrt{2}
6x &= 12\sqrt{2}
x &= \frac{12\sqrt{2}}{6}
x &= 2\sqrt{2}
ANSWER: x = 2\sqrt{2}
